<pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.4" change_id="I4f1a77554e230514bdccdb970e9a7edf8a19fed6" commit="0f49d3f5cde6baa0061c0106e42f5aca3d36a207" commit_message="Revert &quot;HACK: arm64/dts/rockchip AND clk/rockchip: Hack out PWM regulators for rk3399-gru&quot;  This reverts commit 64d6f25328f6478619f156567c084536538af565.  We're ready to specify proper PWM regulator / cpufreq support.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:52666 TEST=test out cpufreq CQ-DEPEND=I11ef54e7e458ea139dca951c7467f069d5720fc6  Change-Id: I4f1a77554e230514bdccdb970e9a7edf8a19fed6 Signed-off-by: Brian Norris &lt;briannorris@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/348052 Reviewed-by: Stephen Barber &lt;smbarber@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="348052" owner_email="briannorris@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/52/348052/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.4" change_id="I11ef54e7e458ea139dca951c7467f069d5720fc6" commit="c7752e06c036c3b970ba2b8e403620e41ae48a70" commit_message="CHROMIUM: ARM64: dts: rockchip: set up OPPs for Gru and Kevin  The DTSI files have plenty of comments about the reasoning here. In short:   - we have early silicon, so there will be another revision of the OPP    table before we stabilize; that's why I split this out into    *-early.dtsi variants. It'll get shared by several boards, but not    all of a given codename (next Gru and next Kevin should each share    the new silicon and voltage characteristics?)  - the 'regulator-microvolt-offset' DT property is slightly buggy (won't    clamp at the maximum supported by the PWM/regulator), and it isn't    quite featureful enough (we want to clamp at 1.2V, not the max 1.5V);    so we just have to copy/paste/modify the whole table for now, I    believe.  As noted in the comments, this trims one OPP off of Kevin since it could potentially exceed the 1.2V maximum, depending on the actual voltage drop on the board.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:52666 TEST=scripts like the following; make sure we don't crash; tested on Gru      rev0 and Kevin rev1   test_cpufreq() {   local cpu_num=${1:-0}   [ &quot;${cpu_num}&quot; -ge 0 ] || return 1   local CPUFREQ_SYSFS=/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy${cpu_num}   echo userspace &gt; ${CPUFREQ_SYSFS}/scaling_governor || return 1   while : ; do    for i in $(cat ${CPUFREQ_SYSFS}/scaling_available_frequencies); do     echo $i &gt; ${CPUFREQ_SYSFS}/scaling_setspeed    done   done  }  test_cpufreq 0 &amp;  test_cpufreq 4 &amp;  # Give our CPUs some load  md5sum /dev/zero &amp;  md5sum /dev/zero &amp;  md5sum /dev/zero &amp;  md5sum /dev/zero &amp;  Change-Id: I11ef54e7e458ea139dca951c7467f069d5720fc6 Signed-off-by: Brian Norris &lt;briannorris@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/348053 Reviewed-by: Stephen Barber &lt;smbarber@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="348053" owner_email="briannorris@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/53/348053/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ibbc62d23d8d909be48a9bec90da8acebb9905b50" commit="a27285226c439b7da5698fc111d2cb5a0bd7df4c" commit_message="reef: Don't set LPC pins in gpio.inc  The default mode for the LPC pins is actually LPC. Setting the altnerate function mode makes them GPIOs.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=build and booted on Reef EC. Didn't seem to make much of a difference on its own but with the follow-up things seemed more stable.  Change-Id: Ibbc62d23d8d909be48a9bec90da8acebb9905b50 Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/347443 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fai